<!--
    kuna castternary (P9, sub-stage cast-policy; option castternary on|off).

    Source: a base64 decoder whose four table lookups `iteregion` prints as
    `a = (c != '=') ? (int4)*(char *)(t + c) : 0;`.  Each conditional arm keeps
    the cast the IR put on it, although the conditional operator converts both
    arms to their common type itself (the usual arithmetic conversions, C11
    6.5.15p5), and the same diamond printed as if/else already has no cast.

    gcc -O0 -fno-pie of these static functions (the bytes below are .text):

      quantum(t, in)  four `in[i] != '=' ? t[(unsigned char)in[i]] : 0`
      ones(p, k)      unsigned int v = k ? p[k] : 0xffffffffu   (unsigned char p)
      both(i, u, k)   long v = k ? (long)i : (long)u            (int i, unsigned int u)
      wide(p, k)      long v = k ? p[k] : 3000000000L           (int p)
      narrow(p, k)    long v = k ? p[k] : -5L                   (int p)
      less(a, b, k)   unsigned int v = k ? (unsigned int)(a < b) : 0xffffffffu

    iteexpr is on in both passes (it is in the aggressive preset kuna selects for
    binaries under 500 KiB) so the load arms print as conditionals.  Pass 1
    (option castternary off) keeps every arm cast: #1, #3, #5, #7.  Pass 2
    (option castternary on) leaves out the widenings the conditional performs
    (#2, #4, #6, #8): the char and unsigned char arms against an int4 or uint4
    constant, the unsigned int arm against an int8 arm whose (int8) stays (#6:
    both bare, int4 and uint4 would meet as uint4), and the int4 arm against a
    decimal literal too wide for int4 (its C type is int8).  Both passes keep
    the (int8) against -5, whose bare conditional would be an int4 (#9), and the
    (uint4) on a comparison (#10).
-->
